Important implication: the validator rejects unknown keys, which conflicts slightly with test D ("adding metadata outside normative projection does not change hash") — if we pass a descriptor with extra own properties, the validator rejects it. So how to test D? We must hash an already-validated descriptor and show that wrapper/ledger/assessment/etc. objects are not parameters — i.e., hash only depends on descriptor. Or test that passing wrapper objects to descriptorHash (cast) yields... hmm, descriptorHash takes descriptor; extra keys rejected by validator only if using validator. descriptorHash itself doesn't validate (per signature). But present-undefined throws. For extra keys, projection ignores them (whitelist). Test D can:
- build descriptor, get hash;
- create wrapper objects (envelope, signature, ledger, assessment, revocation, acceptance, lifecycle) containing copies of descriptor; assert hash of descriptor unchanged;
- and show descriptorHash(wrapper as any) would throw? Not necessarily; whitelist would read d.id etc. absent → undefined → CanonicalizationError. Could assert that.

