Fix both.

For S2871: use `.sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b))`. But Sonar wants "provide a compare function that depends on String.localeCompare". Also expected parents sort.

For complexity: split `collectClosureFailures`. Current:

```ts
function collectClosureFailures(...): Result<Map<...>> {
  const failures = new Map();
  for (const edge of path.edges) {           // +1
    const branches = directParentBranches(graph, edge.child);
    if (!branches.some(...)) continue;        // +1
    for (const branch of branches) {          // +1
      if (branch.parent === edge.parent && branch.relation === edge.relation) continue; // +2 (&&)
      const key = ...
      if (failures.has(key)) continue;        // +1
      const failure = evaluateClosureBranch(...);
      if (!failure.ok) return failure;        // +1
      if (failure.value !== undefined) {      // +1
        failures.set(...)
      }
    }
  }
  return ...
}
```
That's ~8 + nesting increments maybe 18. Extract inner branch evaluation into a helper that returns Result<failure|undefined>, and guard functions.

Proposal:

```ts
function closureBranchesForEdge(graph, edge): EdgeRef[] | undefined {
  const branches = directParentBranches(graph, edge.child);
  if (!branches.some((branch) => branch.relation === 'MERGED_FROM')) return undefined;
  return branches;
}

function recordClosureFailure(
  failures: Map<string, MergeClosureBranchFailure>,
  branch: EdgeRef,
  edge: EdgeRef,
  ...,
): Result<undefined> {
  if (branch.parent === edge.parent && branch.relation === edge.relation) return { ok: true, value: undefined };
  const key = `${...}`;
  if (failures.has(key)) return { ok: true, value: undefined };
  const failure = evaluateClosureBranch(...);
  if (!failure.ok) return failure;
  if (failure.value !== undefined) failures.set(key, failure.value);
  return { ok: true, value: undefined };
}
```

then:

```ts
function collectClosureFailures(...) {
  const failures = new Map(...);
  for (const edge of path.edges) {
    const branches = closureBranchesForEdge(graph, edge);
    if (branches === undefined) continue;
    for (const branch of branches) {
      const collected = recordClosureFailure(failures, branch, edge, assessments, requirement, policy, ctx);
      if (!collected.ok) return collected;
    }
  }
  return { ok: true, value: failures };
}
```

compute `key` inside record.

Also `evaluateClosureBranch` has several ifs; complexity maybe under 15 (it was accepted). Fine.

