Refactor the two mixed tests into a shared helper `expectMixedClosureFailure(options)` used by both. This should remove duplication. Also perhaps merge into one test with a table over two cases (CONTINUATION_ONLY/CONTINUATION; CONTINUATION_AND_FORK/FORKED). Better: one test with `for (const scenario of [...])`. That cuts ~25 lines.

Define:

```ts
function expectMixedClosureFailure(options: {
  scope: LineageTraversalScope;
  entryRelation: EntryRelation;
  expectedRelation: EntryRelation;
  targetParticipant: string;
}) { ... assertions ... }
```

Then one test:
```ts
test('a mixed-relation merge node is closed over MERGED_FROM parents when reached through a permitted edge', () => {
  for (const scenario of [
    { scope: 'CONTINUATION_ONLY', entryRelation: 'CONTINUATION', targetParticipant: 'participant-a' },
    { scope: 'CONTINUATION_AND_FORK', entryRelation: 'FORKED_FROM', targetParticipant: 'participant-d' },
  ] as const) {
    const state = mixedState({ entryRelation: scenario.entryRelation, parentBRelation:'MERGED_FROM', parentCRelation:'MERGED_FROM' });
    ... 
    expect(edges).toEqual([scenario.entryRelation]);
    ... parents ['iv-b','iv-c'], all MERGED, all NO_APPLICABLE.
  }
});
```
